  ('456', 'edusmart_topics_v1', 'top-gr8-pretech-1', '{"id":"top-gr8-pretech-1","grade":8,"subject":"Pretechnical Education","title":"Topic 1: Workshop Safety & Technical Drawing","term":1,"week":2,"description":"Occupational safety protocols, personal protective equipment (PPE), orthographic projection, and drafting instruments."}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_notes_v1', 'note-gr4-math-fractions', '{"id":"note-gr4-math-fractions","topicId":"top-gr4-math-2","grade":4,"subject":"Mathematics","title":"Understanding Fractions: Part of a Whole & Equal Portions","summary":"Master proper fractions, numerators, denominators, and addition of like fractions with real visual models.","content":"\n### 1. What is a Fraction?\nA fraction represents **one or more equal parts of a whole**. \nWhen an item is divided into equal portions, each portion is a fraction.\n\n- **Numerator (Top Number)**: Tells us how many equal parts are selected or considered.\n- **Denominator (Bottom Number)**: Tells us the total number of equal parts the whole is divided into.\n\nExample: In the fraction 3/4:\n- 3 is the **Numerator** (3 parts taken).\n- 4 is the **Denominator** (4 equal parts in total).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Types of Fractions\n1. **Proper Fractions**: The numerator is strictly less than the denominator (e.g., 1/2, 3/4, 5/8).\n2. **Improper Fractions**: The numerator is greater than or equal to the denominator (e.g., 5/3, 7/4).\n3. **Mixed Numbers**: A whole number combined with a proper fraction (e.g., 1 1/2).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Adding Fractions with the Same Denominator\nWhen denominators are identical, simply add the numerators together and keep the denominator unchanged:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 + 1}{2} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1$$\n\n$$\\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{2 + 1}{5} = \\frac{3}{5}$$\n\n---\n\n### 4. Finding a Fraction of a Given Quantity\nTo calculate a fraction of a quantity:\n$$\\text{Fraction} \\times \\text{Total} = \\frac{\\text{Numerator}}{\\text{Denominator}} \\times \\text{Total}$$\n\nExample: Find 3/4 of 20:\n- Step 1: Divide 20 by the denominator 4: $20 \\div 4 = 5$\n- Step 2: Multiply the result by the numerator 3: $5 \\times 3 = 15$\n- **Result: 15**\n    ","keyTakeaways":["The numerator indicates portions taken; the denominator indicates total equal portions.","When adding fractions with identical denominators, add only the numerators.","3/4 of 20 equals 15 by calculating (20 ÷ 4) × 3.","Always simplify fractions to their simplest form where applicable."],"diagramUrl":"/src/assets/images/diagram_math_geometry_1790859731617.jpg","diagramCaption":"Visual representation of fraction circular models and proportional division","author":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-15"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_notes_v1', 'note-gr4-sci-plants', '{"id":"note-gr4-sci-plants","topicId":"top-gr4-sci-1","grade":4,"subject":"Integrated Science","title":"Plant Nutrition: Photosynthesis & Vascular Systems","summary":"Explore how green plants manufacture food using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide through chlorophyll.","content":"\n### 1. Plant Nutrition (Photosynthesis)\nGreen plants are **autotrophs**—they make their own food through the process called **photosynthesis**.\n\nThe word photosynthesis comes from:\n- *Photo* = Light\n- *Synthesis* = Putting together\n\n---\n\n### 2. Requirements for Photosynthesis\nFor photosynthesis to take place, plants require four crucial components:\n1. **Chlorophyll**: Green pigment inside chloroplasts that traps light energy.\n2. **Sunlight**: Natural solar radiation providing energy for the chemical reaction.\n3. **Carbon Dioxide ($CO_2$)**: Absorbed from the atmosphere through microscopic leaf pores called **stomata**.\n4. **Water ($H_2O$)**: Absorbed from soil by root hairs and transported upward via xylem vessels.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Vascular Bundles: Xylem and Phloem\nPlants have specialized transport tubes:\n- **Xylem Vessels**: Conduct water and dissolved minerals from the roots upwards to stems and leaves.\n- **Phloem Vessels**: Translocate manufactured sugars (glucose) from leaves to growing stems, storage roots, and fruits.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Word Equation of Photosynthesis\n$$\\text{Carbon Dioxide} + \\text{Water} \\xrightarrow[\\text{Chlorophyll}]{\\text{Sunlight}} \\text{Glucose (Food)} + \\text{Oxygen}$$\n    ","keyTakeaways":["Photosynthesis produces glucose and releases vital oxygen into the atmosphere.","Xylem conducts water and mineral salts upward from roots to leaves.","Phloem distributes manufactured food from leaves to all other plant parts.","Stomata control gas exchange in plant leaves."],"diagramUrl":"/src/assets/images/diagram_science_ecosystem_1790859717590.jpg","diagramCaption":"Cellular diagram of leaf photosynthesis, chloroplasts, and vascular transport","author":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-18"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_notes_v1', 'note-gr4-math-numbers', '{"id":"note-gr4-math-numbers","topicId":"top-gr4-math-1","grade":4,"subject":"Mathematics","title":"Numbers: Place Value, Total Value & Rounding Off","summary":"Master place values up to ten thousands and learn systematic rounding techniques.","content":"\n### 1. Understanding Place Value\nEvery digit in a number has a value depending on its position:\n- **Ten Thousands (T.Th)**\n- **Thousands (Th)**\n- **Hundreds (H)**\n- **Tens (T)**\n- **Ones (O)**\n\nExample in the number **8,452**:\n- 8 is in Thousands place: Total Value = $8 \\times 1000 = 8000$\n- 4 is in Hundreds place: Total Value = $4 \\times 100 = 400$\n- 5 is in Tens place: Total Value = $5 \\times 10 = 50$\n- 2 is in Ones place: Total Value = $2 \\times 1 = 2$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Rounding Off Numbers\n- **To the nearest 10**: Look at the digit in the Ones place. If it is 5 or greater, round up; if less than 5, round down.\n- **To the nearest 100**: Look at the digit in the Tens place.\n    ","keyTakeaways":["Place value indicates the position of a digit in a numeral.","Total value is the product of the digit and its place value.","Digits 0-4 round down; digits 5-9 round up to the next unit."],"author":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-10"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_notes_v1', 'note-gr8-pretech', '{"id":"note-gr8-pretech","topicId":"top-gr8-pretech-1","grade":8,"subject":"Pretechnical Education","title":"Workshop Safety Protocols & Technical Drafting Tools","summary":"Junior school foundation for occupational health, safety equipment, and orthographic drafting rules.","content":"\n### 1. Workshop Safety Regulations\nA technical workshop requires strict adherence to safety to prevent injuries:\n- **Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)**: Safety goggles, leather aprons, steel-toed boots, and ear defenders.\n- **Fire Precautions**: Familiarity with Carbon Dioxide ($CO_2$), Foam, and Water fire extinguishers.\n- **Tidiness**: Keep walking aisles clear of wood shavings, metal turnings, and electrical cords.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Basic Technical Drafting Instruments\n- **T-Square**: Used for drawing horizontal lines on the drawing board.\n- **Set Squares (45° and 30°/60°)**: Used for precise angled and perpendicular lines.\n- **Divider and Compass**: Used for transferring measurements and striking arcs.\n    ","keyTakeaways":["PPE must be inspected before starting any workshop activity.","Clean workbenches minimize accidents and tool damage.","Drawing instruments must be cleaned and stored in protective cases."],"author":"Mr. Peter Mutua","createdAt":"2026-09-20"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_videos_v1', 'vid-gr4-math-fractions', '{"id":"vid-gr4-math-fractions","topicId":"top-gr4-math-2","grade":4,"subject":"Mathematics","title":"Interactive Video: Visualizing Fractions with Geometric Shapes","durationMinutes":14,"videoUrl":"https://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/gtv-videos-bucket/sample/BigBuckBunny.mp4","videoThumbnail":"/src/assets/images/diagram_math_geometry_1790859731617.jpg","keyPoints":["Visual breakdown of 1/2, 1/4, 3/4 using divided circles and chocolate bar models","Step-by-step method to add fractions with common denominators","Worked examples calculating 3/4 of 20 and 2/3 of 15","Common pitfalls to avoid when simplifying answers"],"teacherName":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-16"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_videos_v1', 'vid-gr4-sci-plants', '{"id":"vid-gr4-sci-plants","topicId":"top-gr4-sci-1","grade":4,"subject":"Integrated Science","title":"Laboratory Demonstration: Investigating Photosynthesis in Green Leaves","durationMinutes":18,"videoUrl":"https://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/gtv-videos-bucket/sample/ElephantsDream.mp4","videoThumbnail":"/src/assets/images/diagram_science_ecosystem_1790859717590.jpg","keyPoints":["Testing a variegated leaf for the presence of starch with iodine solution","Demonstration of oxygen bubbles released by Elodea pondweed under light","Microscope view of open and closed stomatal guard cells","Role of xylem and phloem vessels explained with food dye experiment"],"teacherName":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-19"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_audios_v1', 'aud-gr4-math-fractions', '{"id":"aud-gr4-math-fractions","topicId":"top-gr4-math-2","grade":4,"subject":"Mathematics","title":"Audio Masterclass: Mental Math with Fractions & Decimals","durationMinutes":9,"audioUrl":"https://actions.google.com/sounds/v1/ambiences/outdoor_water_fountain.ogg","transcript":"Welcome Grade 4 scholars to our EduSmart Audio Masterclass. Today we are unlocking mental math shortcuts for fractions. Remember, when someone asks you what is one half plus one half, do not hesitate—two halves make one whole! Now listen carefully to our word challenge: if a farmer has 20 eggs and sells three quarters of them, how many eggs are sold? Divide 20 into four equal baskets: each basket has 5 eggs. Take three baskets: 5 times 3 equals 15! Practice this anytime in your head.","keyPoints":["Quick mental addition of common unit fractions","Associating halves, quarters, and eighths with everyday objects","Audio word problems to sharpen analytical listening"],"teacherName":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-17"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_audios_v1', 'aud-gr4-sci-plants', '{"id":"aud-gr4-sci-plants","topicId":"top-gr4-sci-1","grade":4,"subject":"Integrated Science","title":"Nature Audio Walk: The Secret Life of Plant Roots and Leaves","durationMinutes":11,"audioUrl":"https://actions.google.com/sounds/v1/ambiences/gentle_rain.ogg","transcript":"Step into the school botanical garden with Mr. Kiprono. Close your eyes and listen to the gentle breeze in the canopy. Those emerald green leaves are solar powered food factories. Right now, through tiny mouth-like pores called stomata, carbon dioxide enters the leaf while water ascends all the way from the roots through xylem conduits...","keyPoints":["How plants act as the lungs and food providers of Planet Earth","Sensory identification of tree bark, leaves, and vascular veins","Conservation of indigenous trees in Kenya"],"teacherName":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-21"}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_quizzes_v1', 'quiz-gr4-math-fractions', '{"id":"quiz-gr4-math-fractions","topicId":"top-gr4-math-2","grade":4,"subject":"Mathematics","title":"Mathematics: Fractions Mastery Quiz","passPercentage":70,"timeLimitMinutes":10,"teacherName":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-20","questions":[{"id":"q1","questionText":"What is 1/2 + 1/2 = ?","options":["1","2","1/4","1/3"],"correctOptionIndex":0,"explanation":"1/2 + 1/2 = 2/2, which simplifies to 1 whole."},{"id":"q2","questionText":"Find 3/4 of 20 = ?","options":["12","15","16","18"],"correctOptionIndex":1,"explanation":"Divide 20 by 4 = 5. Then multiply by 3: 5 × 3 = 15."},{"id":"q3","questionText":"Which fraction is equivalent to 2/4?","options":["1/2","1/3","3/4","2/8"],"correctOptionIndex":0,"explanation":"Dividing both numerator and denominator of 2/4 by 2 gives 1/2."},{"id":"q4","questionText":"In the fraction 5/8, what is the number 8 called?","options":["Numerator","Denominator","Quotient","Dividend"],"correctOptionIndex":1,"explanation":"The bottom number in a fraction is the denominator."},{"id":"q5","questionText":"A pupil ate 3/7 of a cake on Monday and 2/7 on Tuesday. What total fraction of cake was eaten?","options":["5/14","5/7","1/7","6/7"],"correctOptionIndex":1,"explanation":"Add numerators: 3/7 + 2/7 = 5/7."}]}'::jsonb, false),
  ('456', 'edusmart_quizzes_v1', 'quiz-gr4-sci-plants', '{"id":"quiz-gr4-sci-plants","topicId":"top-gr4-sci-1","grade":4,"subject":"Integrated Science","title":"Integrated Science: Plant Nutrition & Photosynthesis Quiz","passPercentage":70,"timeLimitMinutes":12,"teacherName":"Mr. David Kiprono","createdAt":"2026-09-22","questions":[{"id":"qs1","questionText":"Which green pigment in leaves absorbs solar energy for photosynthesis?","options":["Chlorophyll","Melanin","Hemoglobin","Keratin"],"correctOptionIndex":0,"explanation":"Chlorophyll is the green pigment in chloroplasts responsible for trapping light."},{"id":"qs2","questionText":"Which vascular tube transports water and mineral salts from roots to leaves?","options":["Phloem","Xylem","Stomata","Cortex"],"correctOptionIndex":1,"explanation":"Xylem conducts water and minerals upward, while phloem transports food."},{"id":"qs3","questionText":"What vital gas is produced and released by green plants during daylight photosynthesis?","options":["Carbon Dioxide","Nitrogen","Oxygen","Methane"],"correctOptionIndex":2,"explanation":"Plants release oxygen ($O_2$) as a byproduct of photosynthesis."}]}'::jsonb, false),
